**Proof.** Place the circumcircle on the complex plane with center at the origin and radius $r$. By scaling we may take $r=1$. Let $\omega = e^{i\pi/3}$, so that multiplication by $\omega$ rotates a point on the unit circle by $60^\circ$.

Since the vertices $A,B,C,D,E,F$ lie on the circle in order and $AB=CD=EF=1$, each of the chords $AB$, $CD$, $EF$ subtends a central angle of $60^\circ$ (because a chord of length $1$ in a unit circle satisfies $2\sin(\theta/2)=1$, giving $\theta=60^\circ$). Hence, traversing the hexagon counterclockwise,

$$B = A\omega,\qquad D = C\omega,\qquad F = E\omega.$$

Set $A=1$ (rotating the whole configuration does not affect the claim). Write $C = \omega u$ and $E = \omega^{2}u\,v$, where $u=e^{ix}$ records the arc $BC$ and $v=e^{iy}$ records the arc $DE$ (with $x,y>0$ and $x+y<180^\circ$ so that the remaining arc $FA$ is positive). Then

$$B=\omega,\quad C=\omega u,\quad D=\omega^{2}u,\quad E=\omega^{2}uv,\quad F=\omega^{3}uv.$$

The midpoints of $\overline{BC}$, $\overline{DE}$, $\overline{FA}$ (up to the irrelevant common factor of $\tfrac12$) are

$$P = B+C = \omega(1+u),$$
$$Q = D+E = \omega^{2}u(1+v),$$
$$R = F+A = \omega^{3}uv + 1 = 1 - uv \quad(\text{since }\omega^{3}=-1).$$

Recall that three points $z_{1},z_{2},z_{3}$ in the complex plane are the vertices of an equilateral triangle if and only if

$$z_{1} + \zeta\, z_{2} + \zeta^{2}\, z_{3} = 0,$$

where $\zeta = e^{2\pi i/3} = \omega^{2}$ is a primitive cube root of unity. (This is the algebraic characterization of an equilateral triangle: it holds for $\{1,\zeta,\zeta^{2}\}$ and is invariant under translation and scaling.)

We verify this condition for $P,Q,R$ with $\zeta=\omega^{2}$ (so $\zeta^{2}=\omega^{4}=-\omega$):

$$P + \omega^{2}Q + \omega^{4}R = \omega(1+u) + \omega^{2}\cdot\omega^{2}u(1+v) + \omega^{4}(1-uv).$$

Using $\omega^{4}=-\omega$ and collecting,

$$= \omega(1+u) + \omega^{4}u(1+v) + \omega^{4}(1-uv)$$
$$= \omega(1+u) + \omega^{4}\bigl[u(1+v) + 1 - uv\bigr]$$
$$= \omega(1+u) + \omega^{4}\bigl[u + uv + 1 - uv\bigr]$$
$$= \omega(1+u) + \omega^{4}(1+u)$$
$$= (1+u)\bigl(\omega + \omega^{4}\bigr) = (1+u)(\omega - \omega) = 0.$$

Thus $P + \omega^{2}Q + \omega^{4}R = 0$, which is precisely the equilateral-triangle condition. Therefore the midpoints of $\overline{BC}$, $\overline{DE}$, and $\overline{FA}$ form the vertices of an equilateral triangle. $\blacksquare$

